```

**Contradictory observation:** The same filter applied to `ge-0/0/0` input should drop new TCP connections to port 443, yet the SYN-ACK from the server is seen in the capture. The capture was taken on the host, so the server's SYN-ACK traverses the MX204 inbound path.

**Your task:** Identify the most likely cause of the intermittent failure, given the contradictory evidence. State what additional read-only evidence you would request from the MX204 to confirm or refute your hypothesis, and explain why the current capture alone is insufficient. Do not propose any configuration changes.

An audit review of the "Meridian Edge" deployment is underway. The edge site runs a pair of Juniper Junos SRX firewalls as the default gateway for the local LAN, using VRRP. The WAN uplink from these firewalls terminates into an Azure virtual network via an ExpressRoute connection to a Microsoft Enterprise Edge (MSEE) device. The Azure side uses a single virtual network gateway (VNG) in Active-Standby mode, with the active instance owning the public IP for the tunnel.

The incident under review: a 40-minute total loss of site-to-cloud connectivity last Tuesday. The LAN remained up, and clients kept their default gateway. The Junos configuration excerpt for the relevant interface is supplied:

```
interfaces {
    ge-0/0/0 {
        unit 0 {
            family inet {
                address 10.10.10.2/24 {
                    vrrp-group 10 {
                        virtual-address 10.10.10.1;
                        priority 150;
                        accept-data;
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

The Azure VNG logs show that the active instance lost BGP peering with the on-premises SRX for 40 minutes, then re-established. The standby Azure instance did not take over. The SRX logs show the BGP session to the VNG went down at the same time, and the VRRP state on the SRX remained MASTER throughout.

The audit question: Was the failover behavior correct, and what is the primary gap in this design? The reviewer expects you to identify that VRRP on the SRX is only tracking the local interface state, not the WAN/BGP reachability. When the ExpressRoute path failed, the SRX did not decrement its VRRP priority, so it stayed MASTER, and the LAN continued to send traffic to a gateway that had no working uplink. The Azure VNG correctly did not fail over because its BGP session to the SRX was down, and it had no reason to promote the standby.

